---
import Replay from '../components/Replay.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import Window from '../components/Window.astro';
import { loadBlocks, loadFacts, loadReplay } from '../diagram.ts';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { released, staged } from '../stage.ts';
const root = process.cwd();
const replay = loadReplay(root);
const facts = loadFacts(root);
const blocks = loadBlocks(root);
const installed = staged('/docs');
const stages = [
	{ name: 'embed', text: 'The current message is embedded on your machine.' },
	{ name: 'search', text: 'Stored turns and facts are searched for candidates.' },
	{ name: 'score', text: 'Each candidate is scored; the ones left out are dropped at a named stage.' },
	{ name: 'compose', text: 'The sections are written into the window in render order.' }
];
const lineage = [
	'Howard, M. W., and Kahana, M. J. (2002). A distributed representation of temporal context. Journal of Mathematical Psychology, 46(3), 269–299.',
	'Anderson, J. R., and Lebiere, C. (1998). The Atomic Components of Thought (ACT-R). Lawrence Erlbaum Associates.',
	'Hintzman, D. L. (1984). MINERVA 2: A simulation model of human memory. Behavior Research Methods, Instruments, and Computers, 16(2), 96–101.',
	"McClelland, J. L., McNaughton, B. L., and O'Reilly, R. C. (1995). Why there are complementary learning systems in the hippocampus and neocortex. Psychological Review, 102(3), 419–457.",
	'Bjork, R. A., and Bjork, E. L. (1992). A new theory of disuse and an old theory of stimulus fluctuation. In A. F. Healy, S. M. Kosslyn and R. M. Shiffrin (Eds.), From Learning Processes to Cognitive Processes, Vol. 2, 35–67. Lawrence Erlbaum Associates.'
];
const parts = ['turn', 'anatomy', 'rebuilt', 'facts', 'rules', 'dates', 'inspect', 'public', 'runtime', 'sources', 'measured'];
/** A section's number in the order this stage shows the sections. */
const numbered = (part: string): string => String(parts.indexOf(part) + 1).padStart(2, '0');
---
<Page title="How it works" description="How Strikta rebuilds the context window before every request, what your runtime still does, and what has been measured.">
	<div class="opening">
		<h1>How Strikta works</h1>
		<p class="lead prose">Every request starts from an empty window. This page shows what fills it, in what order, and what has and has not been measured.</p>
	</div>

	<Section id="turn" number={numbered('turn')} label="A turn" title="A turn, step by step">
		<ol class="flow">
			{stages.map((stage) => <li><span class="node">{stage.name}</span><span class="step">{stage.text}</span></li>)}
		</ol>
	</Section>

	<Section id="anatomy" number={numbered('anatomy')} label="Anatomy" title="What goes into the window">
		<p class="prose">The sections always come in the same order: your agent's instructions, standing rules, the user profile, the memories and facts retrieved for this message, the recent conversation and the message itself. An empty section is left out, except the message.</p>
		<div class="anatomy"><Window turn={replay.to} ranges={installed} caption={`Turn ${replay.to}'s window, in render order. Heights are bytes. Synthetic persona.`} /></div>
	</Section>

	<Section id="rebuilt" number={numbered('rebuilt')} label="Rebuilt every turn" title="The window is rebuilt before every request">
		<p class="prose">Hermes and OpenClaw keep one growing transcript and compact it as it nears the window's limit. Strikta keeps every turn verbatim and assembles a new window for each request: what matters now comes back word for word, and what doesn't is left out of this turn, not lost.</p>
		<Replay replay={replay} />
	</Section>

	<Section id="facts" number={numbered('facts')} label="Facts" title="Facts and corrections">
		<p class="prose">Facts are derived from stored turns and carry the time they were valid. When a fact stops being true, Strikta closes it with an end date, so you can see what the agent believed and when. A fact that was wrong from the start is retracted instead.</p>
		<Timeline facts={facts} caption="Facts of a synthetic persona on one time axis: a superseded fact ends where its successor starts." />
	</Section>

	<Section id="rules" number={numbered('rules')} label="Rules" title="Standing rules">
		<p class="prose">Standing rules are lasting instructions, such as 'always answer in metric units', kept per user. Every turn renders them as one block before the profile and the memories.{released('rules_default') ? '' : ' On in the chatbot preset.'}</p>
		<Terminal text={blocks.rules} label="A standing-rules block" caption="A standing-rules block as the model receives it. Synthetic persona." />
	</Section>

	<Section id="dates" number={numbered('dates')} label="Dates" title="Dates stay attached">
		<p class="prose">Each memory carries its date. Above the retrieved memories the window states the date of the question and tells the model that 'yesterday' in a memory means the day before that memory's date, and to write absolute dates.</p>
		<Terminal text={blocks.dates} label="The date preamble and note" caption="The date preamble and note in the retrieved section. Synthetic persona." />
	</Section>

	<Section id="inspect" number={numbered('inspect')} label="Inspect" title="Inspect and replay">
		<p class="prose">Each assembly writes an audit record: every memory the turn considered, served or not, its score terms, the stage that dropped it, and timings. By default the record holds hashes and counts, not your text.</p>
		<p class="prose">Replay assembles a past turn again, optionally as of an earlier time or with changed settings, as a dry run: nothing is stored. A turn recorded in metadata-only mode cannot be replayed.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section id="public" number={numbered('public')} label="Public repositories" title="Public repositories">
		<p class="prose">In a repository that contains a <code>.public-repo</code> file, Strikta gives the agent only memories from that repository's own sessions: never your profile, other projects or your personal life.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section id="runtime" number={numbered('runtime')} label="Runtimes" title="What your agent's runtime still does">
		<p class="prose">On Hermes, Hermes still compacts its own saved transcript; Strikta journals every request so you can see when its summary reaches the model.</p>
		<p class="prose">On OpenClaw, the plugin has no summariser, and OpenClaw still enforces its prompt budget. When a view runs over that budget, older exchanges drop out of what the model sees first; they stay in the store, and retrieval can bring them back. The plugin supports OpenClaw 2026.9.33 and 2026.10.7; on an older OpenClaw it stays inactive and OpenClaw's own compaction runs.</p>
	</Section>

	<Section id="sources" number={numbered('sources')} label="Sources" title="Where the ideas come from">
		<p class="prose">The engine's design cites these models of human memory. Citing them is not a claim that Strikta reproduces them.</p>
		<ul class="citations prose">
			{lineage.map((reference) => <li>{reference}</li>)}
		</ul>
	</Section>

	<Section id="measured" number={numbered('measured')} label="Measured" title="What is and isn't measured">
		<p class="prose">No ablation has been published, so we do not claim that any single mechanism above improves results. One cited mechanism, MINERVA-style cubing, does not take effect with the default E5 embeddings. Multilingual quality has not been measured. No measurement of Strikta has been published yet.</p>
	</Section>
</Page>
<style>
	.opening h1 {
		margin-block-end: var(--s-space-5);
	}
	.flow {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: subgrid;
		row-gap: var(--s-space-5);
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
	}
	.flow li {
		position: relative;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: var(--s-space-3);
		grid-column: 1 / -1;
	}
	.node {
		justify-self: start;
		padding: var(--s-space-3) var(--s-space-4);
		border: var(--s-line-hair) solid var(--s-color-ink);
		font-family: var(--s-font-mono);
		font-size: var(--s-text-sm);
	}
	.step {
		max-inline-size: var(--s-prose);
		color: var(--s-color-ink-2);
	}
	.anatomy {
		max-inline-size: 24rem;
	}
	.citations {
		padding-inline-start: var(--s-space-5);
	}
	.citations li {
		margin-block-end: var(--s-space-3);
	}
	@media (min-width: 64rem) {
		.flow li {
			grid-column: span 2;
		}
		.flow li + li::before {
			position: absolute;
			inset-block-start: calc(var(--s-space-3) + var(--s-text-sm) * var(--s-leading-body) / 2);
			inset-inline-end: calc(100% + var(--s-line-hair));
			inline-size: var(--s-gutter);
			border-block-start: var(--s-line-hair) solid var(--s-color-ink);
			content: '';
		}
	}
</style>
